{{also|asian|asían}}
==English==
{{wikipedia|Asian people}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|āsiānus}}.

The popular American definition referring to ''[[East Asia|East]]'' and ''[[Southeast Asia|Southeast Asians]]'' collectively seems to be a shortened form of "''[[Asian American]]''", a term whose origins activists and academics trace back to 1968 and [[w: University of California, Berkeley|University of California, Berkeley (UCLA)]] students [[w: Yuji Ichioka|Yuji Ichioka]] and [[w: Emma Gee|Emma Gee]], who, inspired by the Black Power Movement and the protests against the Vietnam War, founded the "Asian American Political Alliance" as a way to unite ''[[Japanese]]'', ''[[Chinese]]'', and ''[[Filipino]]'' American students on campus. It replaced the term "''[[mongoloid|Mongoloid]]''", but still continued the racial grouping of "''Mongoloid''". ''[[South Asia|South Asians]]'' are also sometimes called "''Asian''" in the [[w: Anglosphere#Core_Anglosphere|English settler colonies]] ([[w: US|US]], [[w: Canada|Canada]], [[w: Australia|Australia]], [[w: New Zealand|New Zealand]]) but rarely (if ever) ''[[Near East|Near Easterners]]'' and ''[[Central Asia|Central Asians]]''.

The British term exclusively referring to South Asians was due to South Asians making up a majority of immigrants from the continent of Asia in the United Kingdom.

